  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $c\\cdot d=11$, find the angle between $c=\u27e82t,1\u27e9$ and $d=\u27e82,3\u27e9.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "First, since $c\\cdot d=11$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =11 \\\\ 2t\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3 & =11 \\\\ 4t+3 & =11 \\\\ 4t & =8 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\u27e82(2),1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $c$ and $d$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,c\\,||\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{221}})\u2248{42.27}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
